do you run distributed workloads on  kubernetes or gke if so you are probably  having to manage more and more services  and it's getting harder or harder to  understand what's going on if there are  any issues  this is where a service mesh can help a  service mesh is a dedicated  infrastructure layer that you can add to  your clusters it gives you capabilities  like observability traffic management  and security without having to modify  application code my name is yer i'm an  sre at google cloud and today i'm going  to show you how to use both the open  source version of the istio service mesh  and google's managed antho service mesh  to get better observability for your  services running on gke this is  engineering for reliability  the istio service mesh works by adding  android proxy side cars to your services  running on gke these proxies intercept  all service to service traffic in the  mesh and enable the application aware  features like security traffic  management and routing and observability  this is the data plan the control plane  dynamically programs the proxy side cars  updating them as the rules or the  environment change  your first option for using istio on gke  is to simply install the open source  distribution in your cluster this is the  way to go if you prefer to have the most  control over your deployment or if you  have existing dependencies on features  that may not yet be available in  google's managed offer  istio includes an open source  observability toolkit you can use  prometheus for metrics and zipkin or  jaeger for analyzing tracing you can  also enable proxy access logs and have  the proxy sidecars write out logs for  every request in the mesh let's have a  look at how you can use this toolkit and  centralize all that telemetry to cloud  operations  in our example we have an application  running in the istio demo namespace  we have the istio control plane and  observability components running in the  istio system namespace  this includes prometheus from metrics  and kiali for observing traffic in the  mesh  if for example we want to look at the  request rate for our services in the  mesh  we can use the istioctl dashboard  dashboard prometheus command  [Music]  and run equality like this  if we want to visualize how traffic  flows between the different services in  the mesh we can use kiowa  [Music]  at each node  we can see how much traffic the service  is seeing and what the response time  looks like  finally we can enable request logging  for the envoy sicar proxies by using the  telemetry api  we can then view those logs in the  terminal  now let's have a look at how we can get  this telemetry into cloud operations  to adjust the prometheus metrics we can  actually use google's managed prometheus  binary  just by replacing this image tag  this allows us to query prometheus  metrics in metrics explorer  [Music]  here's that same request right query  the proxy sidecar request logs are  automatically adjusted into cloud  logging  and we can query those too  so through the combination of google's  managed service for prometheus and cloud  logging we can get metric and log  telemetry generated by istio ingested  into cloud operations and use it just  like other telemetry from your  infrastructure and services  if you want to maximize control over  your issue deployment and possibly have  experience with istio already using the  open source distribution is the right  way to go however if you're new to using  a service mesh or would simply like a  more managed experience there's the end  of service mesh or asm it's a tested and  supported distribution of istio giving  you all the benefits of a service mesh  with full google support  if you'd like to control version  upgrades yourself or have existing  integrations you depend on you can  deploy asm with an in-cluster control  plane let's have a look at such a  deployment and how it automatically  sends telemetry to cloud operations  let's first have a look at our cluster  the control plane pods are running in  the istio system namespace just like  what we had when we installed open  source sdm  [Music]  our application pods are also  instrumented with the istio proxy  containers  now let's have a look at the telemetry  that the anta service mesh collects  [Music]  the enthalpy service mesh homepage in  the cloud console has a list of the  services that are running in the mesh in  our cluster  we can select a service and get some  basic information about it  like request rates  error rate  and average latency  the topology view here shows us how the  service sends and receives traffic  the metrics screen allows us to see  these metrics over time broken down by  various dimensions  finally we can get error logs for our  service by going to the diagnostics  screen  we can click out to the logs explorer to  get more details about these error logs  this is also where we can view the  access logs for our services  [Music]  we can get more metric telemetry in  cloud monitor  for example we can run a query like this  to get a breakdown of request rates by  service  so this is how you can use telemetry  automatically ingested from the  in-cluster asm control plane to  understand the behavior and health of  your services  if you're just starting out with istio  or would like to use a fully managed  service you can use managed asm in this  deployment model the control plane is  fully managed by google and does not run  in your cluster it does mean that some  features like custom metric adapters  won't work if you need advanced features  like these the open source distribution  may be the way to go for you if not  let's have a look at a managed asm  deployment and how its telemetry is  automatically ingested into cloud  operations  let's again start by looking at our  cluster  in this case we don't actually have any  istio control plane pods running in the  istio system namespace  the control plane is entirely managed by  google and there are no control plane  components running in the cluster itself  our application pods however are still  instrumented with the istio proxy  sidecar container  now let's have a look at the collected  telemetry  [Music]  just like before we can look at the  services running in our cluster  for all of them we can get the same  basic telemetry including the topology  view  we can get the same metric telemetry  and we can get the same access logs all  of this is made available via the  managed control plane without the need  to have anything running in the cluster  itself  thanks so much for watching today we  learned how a service mesh improves  observability for distributed workloads  around gke and how you can use cloud  operations to get telemetry from both  open source istio and the enthalpy  service mesh to observe your own  services i hope this helps you to keep  your applications reliable and your  users happy don't forget to like and  subscribe to never miss out on more  engineering for reliability with google  cloud see you soon  [Music]  you
 

